# TypeScript Standards

## Strict Mode

- **No `any`** — Use `unknown` with type guards when type is uncertain.
- **Explicit prop interfaces** — Define `interface Props` for component props.
- **Named exports** — Prefer `export const foo` over `export default`.

## Type Inference

- **Prefer inferred types.** Do not add explicit types when TypeScript infers correctly.
- **Avoid redundant annotations:**
  - Variables: `const x = "foo"` not `const x: string = "foo"`
  - Function returns: omit when obvious — `function getId() { return "abc" }` not `function getId(): string { ... }`
  - Parameters in simple cases when inference flows from usage
- **Add explicit types when:**
  - Public API: exported component props (`interface Props`)
  - Server Action inputs/outputs (clarity for callers)
  - Complex generics or type guards
  - Return type aids readability for non-trivial functions

## Examples

```ts
// ❌ Bad
function parse(data: any) {
  return data.value;
}

// ✅ Good
function parse(data: unknown): { value: string } {
  if (typeof data === "object" && data !== null && "value" in data) {
    return { value: String((data as { value: unknown }).value) };
  }
  throw new Error("Invalid data");
}
```

```tsx
// ❌ Bad
export default function Card({ title }: { title: string }) {
  return <div>{title}</div>;
}

// ✅ Good
interface CardProps {
  title: string;
}

export function Card({ title }: CardProps) {
  return <div>{title}</div>;
}
```
